<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solway Firth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Solway Firth is an inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> that forms part of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a> and Dumfries and Galloway. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>, begun around 122 CE, ran from the Tyne in the east to Bowness-on-Solway in the west, so the firth marked the western end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s main northern frontier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solway-firth.jpg" alt="Solway Firth" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phillip Capper from Wellington, New Zealand · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solway_Firth,_England-Scotland,_24_Sept._2011_-_Flickr_-_PhillipC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Solway Firth divides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>, including the Solway Plain, from Dumfries and Galloway, and lies close to the Isle of Man. It is the estuary of the River Eden and the River Esk. Its coast is mainly rural, with lowland hills and small mountains, and fishing, hill farming and some arable farming matter to the local economy. Salt marshes and mud flats within the firth can be dangerous because of shifting patches of quicksand. It has few islands, among them Hestan Island, Rough Island, Little Ross and the Islands of Fleet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Solway, recorded as Sulewad in 1218, is of Scandinavian origin and was first the name of a ford across the mud flats at Eskmouth. Its second element is the Old Norse vað, meaning ford, and its first is probably from súl, meaning pillar, referring to the Lochmaben Stane, though súla, a solan goose, is also possible. The area had three fords: the Annan or Bowness Wath, the Dornock Wath and the main one, the Solewath.</p><p>A wooden lighthouse was built at Barnkirk Point in 1841 and destroyed by fire in 1960. Between 1869 and 1921 the Solway Junction Railway crossed the estuary on an iron viaduct 1,780 metres long, built to carry iron ore from the Whitehaven area to Lanarkshire. The railway was not a financial success. After it closed the viaduct became a popular footpath, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> was dry on Sundays and alcohol was legally available in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and it was demolished between 1931 and 1933.</p><p>Margaret Wilson, a Scottish Covenanter who refused to acknowledge James II as head of the church, was executed in 1685 by drowning in the firth. She was tied to a stake in the water and left as the tide came in. John Everett Millais depicted her in a wood engraving published in 1862.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Parts of the English coast were designated the Solway Coast Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty in 1964, and there are national nature reserves at Caerlaverock and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>. Construction of the Robin Rigg Wind Farm in the firth began in 2007. The Annandale Way, a walking route of 85 kilometres from the Moffat Hills, opened in September 2009 and ends at the firth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Solway Firth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hadrian's Wall, begun around 122 and running some 73 miles from the Tyne to the <b>Solway</b>, became the main frontier; the Antonine Wall between the Forth and the Clyde, built from about 142, was held for only some twenty years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The wall ran for 80 Roman miles, equivalent to 73 modern miles or 117 kilometres, from Segedunum at Wallsend on the River Tyne, via Carlisle and Kirkandrews-on-Eden, to the shore of the <b>Solway Firth</b>, ending a short but unknown distance west of Bowness-on-Solway.</blockquote><a href="/work/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
